    # 168 - 209: Tissue Systems & Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf (42 questions)
    anatomy = [
        (168, "Why does the Casparian strip of root endodermal cells force water to enter the symplastic pathway?",
         {"A": "Because suberin is hydrophobic and completely blocks the apoplastic movement of water through cell walls and intercellular spaces", "B": "Because Casparian strips actively hydrolyze water into ions", "C": "Because the strip closes all plasmodesmata", "D": "Because the endodermis lacks living protoplasts"},
         "A", "Casparian Strip Apoplast Block.",
         "1. The Casparian strip consists of suberin bands impregnated in the radial and transverse walls of endodermal cells.\n2. Water moving freely through the porous cell walls (apoplast) cannot cross the waxy suberin barrier, forcing water across the plasma membrane into the living symplasm, enabling active selective regulation.",
         "Options B, C, and D are scientifically incorrect.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Casparian Strip Barrier Mechanism"),

        (169, "In an endodermis with Casparian strips, certain thin-walled cells located directly opposite to the protoxylem points lack suberin thickenings. These cells are called:",
         {"A": "Passage cells (transfusion cells)", "B": "Bulliform cells", "C": "Subsidiary cells", "D": "Guard cells"},
         "A", "Passage Cells in Endodermis.",
         "1. In older roots where endodermal cells develop secondary suberin and tertiary U-shaped lignin thickenings, passage cells opposite protoxylem vessels remain unthickened.\n2. They allow water and minerals to flow from cortex into xylem without hindrance.",
         "Bulliform cells roll leaves. Subsidiary cells surround stomata.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Passage Cells"),

        (170, "Why is the origin of lateral roots described as 'endogenous'?",
         {"A": "Because they originate deep inside the root from the pericycle layer, internal to the endodermis", "B": "Because they develop from the outer epidermis", "C": "Because they are initiated by fungal mycorrhizae", "D": "Because they grow inward towards the pith"},
         "A", "Endogenous Origin of Lateral Roots.",
         "1. Lateral roots develop by localized periclinal and anticlinal cell divisions of the pericycle internal to the endodermis.\n2. They push through the cortex and epidermis to emerge outward; hence their origin is deep/endogenous.\n3. Shoot branches develop exogenously from superficial axillary buds.",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Endogenous Root Origin"),

        (171, "In a dicotyledonous root, what provides the initial trigger for secondary growth?",
         {"A": "Parenchyma cells of the conjunctive tissue beneath the phloem patches become meristematic", "B": "The root cap divides to form phelloderm", "C": "Epidermal root hairs fuse to form cambium", "D": "Endodermis cells detach from cortex"},
         "A", "Initiation of Secondary Growth in Dicot Root.",
         "1. Secondary growth begins when conjunctive parenchyma cells lying immediately below phloem bundles dedifferentiate into strips of vascular cambium.\n2. Later, pericycle cells above protoxylem join them to complete the wavy ring.",
         "Options B, C, and D are incorrect.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Dicot Root Secondary Initiation"),

        (172, "How do the vascular bundles of a dicot stem differ from those of a monocot stem?",
         {"A": "Dicot stem: arranged in a ring, open (cambium present), wedge-shaped; Monocot stem: scattered in ground tissue, closed (no cambium), oval with bundle sheath", "B": "Dicot stem: scattered, closed; Monocot stem: ring, open", "C": "Dicot stem: radial, exarch; Monocot stem: conjoint, endarch", "D": "Both have identical ring arrangements"},
         "A", "Dicot Stem vs Monocot Stem Bundles.",
         "1. Dicot stem vascular bundles are arranged in a ring, conjoint, collateral, open (intrafascicular cambium present).\n2. Monocot stem vascular bundles are scattered throughout ground tissue, conjoint, collateral, closed (no cambium), and enclosed by a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath.",
         "Option B reverses the diagnostic facts.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Dicot vs Monocot Stem Bundles"),

        (173, "In a monocot stem, the vascular bundles are surrounded by a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath. Why are peripheral bundles smaller than central bundles?",
         {"A": "Peripheral bundles contain less metaxylem and more mechanical sclerenchyma fibres to resist external bending stress", "B": "Peripheral bundles are older and undergoing senescence", "C": "Central bundles receive more sunlight", "D": "Peripheral bundles lack phloem"},
         "A", "Biomechanical Significance of Monocot Bundle Gradient.",
         "1. In monocots, hollow cylindrical or columnar engineering requires rigid outer walls.\n2. Small, densely crowded peripheral vascular bundles embedded in sclerenchyma maximize resistance to wind bending and bucking (like a reinforced concrete pipe).",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Monocot Bundle Size Biomechanics"),

        (174, "In which plant organ would you find a starch sheath, semilunar sclerenchymatous pericycle patches, and conjoint open vascular bundles in a ring?",
         {"A": "Dicotyledonous stem (e.g., Helianthus / sunflower)", "B": "Monocotyledonous stem (e.g., Zea mays)", "C": "Dicotyledonous root", "D": "Monocotyledonous root"},
         "A", "Dicot Stem Hallmarks.",
         "1. A starch sheath (endodermis rich in starch), sclerenchymatous pericycle above vascular bundles, and a ring of open bundles are unique anatomical hallmarks of a dicot stem (sunflower).",
         "Monocot stem has scattered closed bundles and lacks a starch sheath.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Dicot Stem Anatomy Hallmarks"),

        (175, "In which plant organ would you observe scattered, closed vascular bundles, a sclerenchymatous hypodermis, and a large undifferentiated parenchymatous ground tissue?",
         {"A": "Monocotyledonous stem (e.g., maize)", "B": "Dicotyledonous stem", "C": "Dicotyledonous leaf", "D": "Monocotyledonous root"},
         "A", "Monocot Stem Hallmarks.",
         "1. A monocot stem (maize) is diagnosed by a sclerenchymatous hypodermis, extensive ground tissue lacking distinct cortex/pith differentiation, and scattered closed vascular bundles with sclerenchyma sheaths.",
         "Dicot stem has ring of open bundles.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Monocot Stem Anatomy Hallmarks"),

        (176, "In a dorsiventral (dicot) leaf, why is the spongy mesophyll loosely arranged with numerous intercellular spaces and large air cavities communicating with stomata?",
         {"A": "To facilitate rapid diffusion of carbon dioxide and oxygen between the atmosphere and photosynthetic palisade cells", "B": "To store heavy mineral salts", "C": "To prevent water from reaching the upper surface", "D": "To produce secondary wood"},
         "A", "Spongy Mesophyll Gas Exchange.",
         "1. Spongy mesophyll cells are loosely arranged with extensive intercellular spaces connected directly to substomatal chambers.\n2. This creates a vast internal surface area for rapid CO2 uptake and O2 exit during photosynthesis.",
         "Options B, C, and D are incorrect.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Spongy Mesophyll Adaptation"),

        (177, "In a dicot leaf, palisade parenchyma cells are packed with chloroplasts and arranged perpendicular to the upper epidermis. What is the physiological advantage of this vertical orientation?",
         {"A": "It allows sunlight to penetrate deep into the leaf while chloroplasts can move along lateral walls to avoid photo-damage under intense light", "B": "It prevents stomata from closing at night", "C": "It stores starch inside the Casparian strip", "D": "It secretes waxy cuticle onto the lower surface"},
         "A", "Palisade Orientation Advantage.",
         "1. The columnar vertical orientation of palisade cells creates 'light guides' channeling solar photons deep into the mesophyll.\n2. Chloroplasts can relocate along the vertical lateral walls (cyclosis) to maximize absorption under low light or avoid photoinhibition under high irradiance.",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Palisade Cell Orientation"),

        (178, "In C4 plants (e.g., maize, sugarcane), the anatomical specialization where vascular bundles are surrounded by large, wreath-like bundle sheath cells packed with agranal chloroplasts is called:",
         {"A": "Kranz anatomy", "B": "Atactostele", "C": "Eustele", "D": "Velamen anatomy"},
         "A", "Kranz Anatomy in C4 Plants.",
         "1. Kranz anatomy ('Kranz' = wreath/crown in German) is the structural hallmark of C4 photosynthesis.\n2. Bundle sheath cells form concentric layers around veins, possess thick suberized walls without intercellular spaces, and contain large agranal chloroplasts that concentrate CO2 for RuBisCO.",
         "Atactostele is scattered bundles. Eustele is ring of bundles.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Kranz Anatomy"),

        (179, "How do bundle sheath cells of C4 plants differ anatomically from adjacent mesophyll cells?",
         {"A": "Bundle sheath cells are large, tightly packed with thick impermeable walls, rich in agranal chloroplasts, and lack intercellular spaces", "B": "Bundle sheath cells have no chloroplasts at all", "C": "Bundle sheath cells are dead sclereids", "D": "Bundle sheath cells perform light reactions only and lack RuBisCO"},
         "A", "Bundle Sheath Cell Characteristics.",
         "1. Bundle sheath cells in C4 leaves have thick, gas-impermeable walls, no intercellular spaces, and large agranal chloroplasts containing RuBisCO and Calvin cycle enzymes.\n2. Mesophyll cells contain granal chloroplasts with PEP carboxylase.",
         "Options B, C, and D are factually erroneous.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "C4 Bundle Sheath Traits"),

        (180, "In leaves of grass plants, where are the large, empty, colorless bulliform cells located?",
         {"A": "On the adaxial (upper) epidermis along the veins", "B": "On the abaxial (lower) epidermis only", "C": "Inside the xylem vessels", "D": "In the Casparian strips"},
         "A", "Bulliform Cell Localization.",
         "1. Bulliform or motor cells are modified adaxial (upper) epidermal cells occurring in longitudinal bands along the veins of grass leaves.",
         "They are not found on the abaxial epidermis or in vascular tissues.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Bulliform Localization"),

        (181, "What causes water drops to exude from the tips of grass blades and margins of leaves in garden nasturtium early in the morning (guttation)?",
         {"A": "High root pressure developing under conditions of high soil moisture and low atmospheric transpiration", "B": "Intense transpiration pull on sunny afternoons", "C": "Breakdown of guard cell chloroplasts", "D": "Active pumping of sucrose into phloem"},
         "A", "Mechanism of Guttation.",
         "1. At night and early morning, transpiration is minimal due to high humidity and closed stomata.\n2. Continued active ion absorption by roots drives water into xylem, building positive hydrostatic root pressure that forces liquid water out through specialized vein-ending pores called hydathodes (guttation).",
         "Options B, C, and D do not cause guttation.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Guttation Mechanism"),

        (182, "Hydathodes differ fundamentally from stomata because:",
         {"A": "Hydathodes are permanent open pores located at vein endings lacking active closure mechanisms, exuding liquid water", "B": "Hydathodes have active guard cells that open and close daily", "C": "Hydathodes release water vapor exclusively", "D": "Hydathodes are found only on underground roots"},
         "A", "Hydathodes vs Stomata.",
         "1. Stomata have living guard cells that regulate pore aperture, losing pure water vapor during transpiration.\n2. Hydathodes are permanent non-closing pores connected to loose epithem parenchyma at vein endings, exuding guttation liquid (water with dissolved minerals).",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Hydathodes vs Stomata"),

        (183, "In a stomatal apparatus, when specialized epidermal cells adjacent to the guard cells become distinct in shape and size from normal epidermal cells, they are called:",
         {"A": "Subsidiary cells (accessory cells)", "B": "Bulliform cells", "C": "Complementary cells", "D": "Lodicules"},
         "A", "Subsidiary Cells.",
         "1. Subsidiary or accessory cells are specialized epidermal cells flanking guard cells that serve as dynamic reservoirs for water and potassium ions during stomatal opening and closing.",
         "Bulliform cells roll leaves. Complementary cells occur in lenticels.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Subsidiary Cells"),

        (184, "During stomatal opening, what causes guard cells to curve outward and widen the stomatal aperture?",
         {"A": "Active influx of K+ ions and water causes turgor pressure to stretch the thin outer walls outward, pulling the thick inelastic inner walls apart", "B": "Water loss makes the guard cells completely flaccid", "C": "Lignification of the inner wall forces the pore open", "D": "The pore is forced open by the upward growth of root hairs"},
         "A", "Biophysical Mechanism of Stomatal Opening.",
         "1. Guard cells have thin, elastic outer walls and thick, inelastic inner concave walls with radial cellulose microfibrils.\n2. When turgid due to K+ and malate accumulation, the outer walls bulge outward, pulling the inner walls with them and opening the pore.",
         "Option B causes stomatal closure.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Stomatal Opening Biophysics"),

        (185, "The radial arrangement of cellulose microfibrils in the cell walls of kidney-shaped guard cells facilitates:",
         {"A": "Radial expansion during turgor increase, forcing the pore open longitudinally", "B": "Longitudinal expansion while preventing transverse stretching", "C": "Waterproofing of the stomatal pore", "D": "Photosynthesis without chloroplasts"},
         "A", "Cellulose Microfibril Orientation in Guard Cells.",
         "1. Cellulose microfibrils are oriented radially (hoop reinforcement) around the circumference of guard cells.\n2. This prevents lateral expansion in width, converting turgor increase into longitudinal bowing that pulls the inner walls apart, opening the aperture.",
         "Options B, C, and D are incorrect.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Guard Cell Microfibrils"),

        (186, "In anomocytic (ranunculaceous) stomata, the guard cells are:",
         {"A": "Surrounded by epidermal cells that do not differ in size or shape from the rest of the epidermal cells", "B": "Enclosed by exactly two subsidiary cells with their common wall perpendicular to the pore", "C": "Surrounded by three subsidiary cells, of which one is distinctly smaller", "D": "Accompanied by two dumb-bell shaped cells"},
         "A", "Anomocytic Stomata.",
         "1. Anomocytic (irregular-celled) stomata have guard cells surrounded by ordinary epidermal cells with no specialized subsidiary cells (e.g., Ranunculaceae, Malvaceae).",
         "Diacytic has two perpendicular cells. Anisocytic has three unequal cells.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Anomocytic Stomata"),

        (187, "In anisocytic (cruciferous) stomata, the guard cells are surrounded by:",
         {"A": "Three subsidiary cells, of which one is distinctly smaller than the other two", "B": "Two equal subsidiary cells parallel to the pore", "C": "A complete ring of identical subsidiary cells", "D": "No subsidiary cells at all"},
         "A", "Anisocytic Stomata in Brassicaceae.",
         "1. Anisocytic (unequal-celled) stomata possess three subsidiary cells, where one cell is noticeably smaller than the other two (characteristic of Brassicaceae / Cruciferae).",
         "Paracytic has two parallel cells. Diacytic has two perpendicular cells.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Anisocytic Stomata"),

        (188, "In paracytic (rubiaceous) stomata, the guard cells are accompanied by two subsidiary cells whose long axes are:",
         {"A": "Parallel to the long axis of the guard cells and stomatal pore", "B": "Perpendicular to the pore", "C": "Spiral in arrangement", "D": "Radially disposed like wheel spokes"},
         "A", "Paracytic Stomata.",
         "1. In paracytic (parallel-celled) stomata, one or more subsidiary cells lie on each side of the stoma with their longitudinal axes parallel to the guard cells (characteristic of Rubiaceae and Fabaceae).",
         "Option B describes diacytic stomata.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Paracytic Stomata"),

        (189, "In diacytic (caryophyllaceous) stomata, the guard cells are enclosed by two subsidiary cells whose common transverse wall is oriented:",
         {"A": "At right angles (perpendicular) to the long axis of the stomatal pore", "B": "Parallel to the stoma", "C": "Diagonal at 45 degrees", "D": "Concentric around the pore"},
         "A", "Diacytic Stomata.",
         "1. In diacytic (cross-celled) stomata, the stoma is surrounded by two subsidiary cells whose common cell wall is at right angles (perpendicular) to the longitudinal axis of the guard cells (characteristic of Caryophyllaceae, Ocimum).",
         "Option B describes paracytic.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Diacytic Stomata"),

        (190, "In a dicot stem transverse section, what biochemical test confirms that the endodermal layer is the 'starch sheath'?",
         {"A": "Staining with Lugol's iodine turns the abundant starch grains inside endodermal cells dark blue-black", "B": "Staining with Sudan III turns the cells red", "C": "Treatment with phloroglucinol-HCl turns cells bright pink", "D": "Burning the tissue creates a smell of burning hair"},
         "A", "Starch Sheath Iodine Test.",
         "1. The endodermis of dicot stems contains dense amyloplasts packed with starch.\n2. Staining cross-sections with potassium iodide (IKI) yields a diagnostic blue-black ring demarcating the starch sheath.",
         "Sudan III tests lipids. Phloroglucinol tests lignin.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Starch Sheath Test"),

        (191, "In the primary vascular bundle of a dicot stem, the intrafascicular cambium is considered primary in origin because:",
         {"A": "It originates directly from the embryonic procambium of the shoot apical meristem", "B": "It develops by dedifferentiation of cortical parenchyma", "C": "It produces secondary cork", "D": "It arises from the pericycle"},
         "A", "Primary Nature of Intrafascicular Cambium.",
         "1. Intrafascicular cambium is a residual strip of undifferentiated embryonic procambium that persists between primary xylem and primary phloem within vascular bundles; hence it is primary in origin.\n2. Interfascicular cambium, by contrast, arises secondarily by dedifferentiation of medullary rays.",
         "Option B describes interfascicular cambium.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Intrafascicular Cambium Origin"),

        (192, "Why are monocot stem vascular bundles described as 'collateral and closed'?",
         {"A": "Phloem lies on the outer side of xylem along the same radius, and no cambium is present between them", "B": "Xylem completely surrounds phloem", "C": "Phloem completely surrounds xylem", "D": "Cambium forms secondary xylem towards the outside"},
         "A", "Collateral Closed Vascular Bundle.",
         "1. Collateral: Phloem lies on the outer radius and xylem on the inner radius.\n2. Closed: Cambium is completely absent between xylem and phloem, preventing secondary growth.",
         "Options B and C describe concentric bundles.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Collateral Closed Definition"),

        (193, "Bicollateral vascular bundles, where phloem is present on both the outer and inner sides of xylem with two strips of cambium, are characteristic of family:",
         {"A": "Cucurbitaceae (pumpkin, cucumber) and Solanaceae", "B": "Poaceae (grasses)", "C": "Brassicaceae", "D": "Liliaceae"},
         "A", "Bicollateral Bundles in Cucurbitaceae.",
         "1. In bicollateral vascular bundles, the sequence from outside to inside is: Outer phloem -> Outer cambium -> Xylem -> Inner cambium -> Inner phloem.\n2. Characteristic of family Cucurbitaceae and some Solanaceae.",
         "Options B, C, and D have collateral bundles.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Bicollateral Bundles"),

        (194, "Concentric amphicribral (hadrocentric) vascular bundles, where phloem completely surrounds a central xylem core, are typically found in:",
         {"A": "Fern rhizomes (pteridophytes)", "B": "Dicot stems", "C": "Monocot stems", "D": "Gymnosperm roots"},
         "A", "Amphicribral Bundles in Ferns.",
         "1. Amphicribral (hadrocentric) bundles have xylem at the center surrounded entirely by a cylinder of phloem, typical of fern rhizomes (Polypodium, Pteris).",
         "Amphivasal (leptocentric) bundles have phloem at the center surrounded by xylem (Dracaena).",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Amphicribral Bundles"),

        (195, "Concentric amphivasal (leptocentric) vascular bundles, where xylem completely surrounds a central phloem core, are found in anomalous monocots like:",
         {"A": "Dracaena and Yucca", "B": "Sunflower", "C": "Maize", "D": "Ferns"},
         "A", "Amphivasal Bundles in Dracaena.",
         "1. Amphivasal (leptocentric) bundles have a central phloem core surrounded completely by a ring of xylem, characteristic of stems of Dracaena and Yucca.",
         "Maize has collateral closed bundles.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Amphivasal Bundles"),

        (196, "In a dicot root, how do lateral roots navigate through the cortical parenchyma without mechanically tearing it to shreds?",
         {"A": "The growing lateral root tip secretes cell-wall-loosening and hydrolytic enzymes (pectinases, cellulases) to dissolve a pathway through the cortex", "B": "It grows through air cavities in aerenchyma", "C": "The cortex dissolves autolytically ahead of time", "D": "Lateral roots have sharp silica drills at their apex"},
         "A", "Lateral Root Emergence Enzymology.",
         "1. The lateral root primordium originating from the pericycle secretes cell wall-remodeling enzymes (pectate lyases, endoglucanases).\n2. These dissolve middle lamellae of overlying cortical and epidermal cells, allowing smooth emergence.",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Lateral Root Emergence"),

        (197, "In an isobilateral monocot leaf, how does the cuticle thickness compare between the adaxial (upper) and abaxial (lower) surfaces?",
         {"A": "Cuticle thickness is almost equal on both surfaces because the leaf is held vertically and receives equal illumination", "B": "The upper surface has a cuticle 100 times thicker", "C": "The lower surface has no cuticle at all", "D": "Both surfaces lack cuticle entirely"},
         "A", "Cuticle Symmetry in Isobilateral Leaf.",
         "1. Isobilateral leaves are oriented vertically, exposing both faces to similar environmental conditions (solar irradiance, wind turbulence).\n2. Consequently, cuticle thickness and stomatal density are nearly symmetrical on adaxial and abaxial surfaces.",
         "Dorsiventral leaves have thicker cuticle on the upper sun-exposed surface.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Isobilateral Cuticle Symmetry"),

        (198, "In Nerium (oleander), an adaptation to extreme drought includes sunken stomata located inside deep epidermal depressions called stomatal crypts lined with hairs. What is their function?",
         {"A": "Trap humid air within the crypt, drastically reducing the water vapor concentration gradient and transpirational loss", "B": "Attract nighttime dew directly into phloem", "C": "Facilitate nocturnal salt excretion", "D": "Absorb solar heat to warm the leaves"},
         "A", "Stomatal Crypts in Nerium.",
         "1. Nerium has sunken stomata restricted to deep cavities (stomatal crypts) lined with epidermal trichomes.\n2. This maintains a thick, stagnant layer of high-humidity air over stomatal pores, preventing dry winds from stripping moisture.",
         "Options B, C, and D are incorrect.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Stomatal Crypts"),

        (199, "What constitutes the 'exodermis' in older roots of many angiosperms?",
         {"A": "A subepidermal cortical layer whose cells develop suberized Casparian-like thickenings to replace a dying epiblema", "B": "The outer layer of the root cap", "C": "The secondary xylem vessels", "D": "The swollen pulvinus"},
         "A", "Exodermis of Root.",
         "1. In older root zones where epiblema and root hairs die and slough off, the outermost layer of the cortex suberizes and develops Casparian bands.\n2. This specialized layer is the exodermis, serving as a protective waterproof and pathogen barrier.",
         "Options B, C, and D are unrelated.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Root Exodermis"),

        (200, "In transverse section of a dicot stem, medullary rays are described as primary rays because:",
         {"A": "They are formed by primary ground parenchyma between the primary vascular bundles during primary shoot development", "B": "They produce primary root hairs", "C": "They are made of primary wood vessels", "D": "They disappear completely after seed germination"},
         "A", "Primary Medullary Rays.",
         "1. Primary medullary rays are the radial parenchymatous bands of ground tissue extending from the central pith to the pericycle between adjacent primary vascular bundles.\n2. Secondary rays are formed later by the ray initials of vascular cambium.",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Primary Medullary Rays"),

        (201, "Why does a ring arrangement of vascular bundles in dicot stems facilitate the formation of a continuous vascular cambium ring?",
         {"A": "The intrafascicular cambium strips within bundles lie in the same geometric cylinder as the adjacent medullary rays, allowing seamless circular connection", "B": "Because vascular bundles are scattered randomly", "C": "Because the stem has no cortex", "D": "Because the phloem is situated in the center"},
         "A", "Geometry of Cambial Ring Formation.",
         "1. Because dicot bundles are arranged in a circular ring, their intrafascicular cambium strips align in the same radial cylinder.\n2. Dedifferentiation of intervening medullary ray cells creates interfascicular strips that bridge the gaps smoothly into a perfect circle.",
         "Scattered bundles in monocots cannot form a simple continuous ring.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Cambial Ring Geometry"),

        (202, "In a leaf, veins provide which two essential physiological and mechanical functions?",
         {"A": "Conduction of water/minerals and organic nutrients, and skeletal structural framework rigidity to the lamina", "B": "Formation of annual growth rings and cork", "C": "Absorption of soil groundwater directly", "D": "Attraction of pollinators through floral pigments"},
         "A", "Dual Functions of Leaf Veins.",
         "1. Conduction: Xylem in veins brings water and minerals from roots; phloem translocates photosynthates to sinks.\n2. Support: Rigid lignified xylem and bundle sheath fibres act as skeletal ribs that keep the flexible leaf lamina expanded flat.",
         "Options B, C, and D are not leaf vein functions.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Functions of Leaf Veins"),

        (203, "In dicot leaves, the size of vascular bundles is dependent on the:",
         {"A": "Size of the veins (larger in midrib and main lateral veins, smaller in veinlets)", "B": "Height of the tree above sea level", "C": "Presence of root caps", "D": "Color of floral petals"},
         "A", "Vascular Bundle Size Variation in Dicot Leaf.",
         "1. In dicot leaves with reticulate venation, vein diameter varies widely.\n2. The main midrib and prominent lateral veins house large vascular bundles, while fine reticulate veinlets contain minute bundles.",
         "In monocot parallel venation, bundle sizes are nearly uniform.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Dicot Vein Bundle Size"),

        (204, "Which of the following tissues is characterized by cells with dense cytoplasm, large nuclei, no vacuoles, and extremely thin primary cell walls?",
         {"A": "Meristematic tissue (shoot/root apex)", "B": "Sclerenchyma fibres", "C": "Collenchyma of hypodermis", "D": "Phellem (cork)"},
         "A", "Cytological Characteristics of Meristematic Cells.",
         "1. Actively dividing meristematic cells have dense granular cytoplasm, prominent nuclei, thin cellulose walls with high plasticity, and either lack vacuoles or possess minute provacuoles.",
         "Options B and D are dead cells. Collenchyma has large central vacuoles.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Meristematic Cytology"),

        (205, "The endodermis in roots acts as a biological checkpoint primarily because:",
         {"A": "The Casparian strip forces all water and solutes to cross the selectively permeable plasma membrane of endodermal cells", "B": "It secretes hydrochloric acid into the soil", "C": "It produces secondary xylem", "D": "It kills harmful mycorrhizal fungi"},
         "A", "Endodermal Checkpoint.",
         "1. By blocking the non-selective apoplastic pathway via suberized Casparian strips, the root forces all soil solutions through the plasma membrane of endodermal cells.\n2. Membrane transport proteins selectively admit beneficial nutrient ions while excluding toxins.",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Endodermal Checkpoint"),

        (206, "In an old dicot root, where does the cork cambium (phellogen) originate during periderm formation?",
         {"A": "Pericycle layer", "B": "Epidermis", "C": "Endodermis", "D": "Primary xylem vessels"},
         "A", "Origin of Cork Cambium in Dicot Root.",
         "1. In dicot roots, as secondary vascular tissues expand, the cortex and endodermis are sloughed off.\n2. The cork cambium (phellogen) arises secondarily from the outer cells of the pericycle.",
         "In stems, phellogen arises in the outer cortex or hypodermis.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Root Cork Cambium Origin"),

        (207, "In monocot roots, why is there no cambium formation from conjunctive tissue?",
         {"A": "Conjunctive parenchyma cells in monocots do not possess the genetic or developmental competence to dedifferentiate into meristems", "B": "Monocot roots lack xylem and phloem", "C": "Monocot roots are covered by cork from germination", "D": "Monocot roots are always aerial"},
         "A", "Lack of Cambium Competence in Monocots.",
         "1. Monocot root tissues undergo complete permanent terminal differentiation.\n2. Conjunctive tissue cells lack the capacity to dedifferentiate into secondary meristematic initials, precluding secondary vascular development.",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Monocot Cambium Incompetence"),

        (208, "The presence of a parenchymatous pericycle in roots vs a sclerenchymatous pericycle in dicot stems is functionally related to the fact that:",
         {"A": "Root pericycle must remain living to initiate lateral roots and cambium, while stem pericycle primarily provides mechanical support", "B": "Root pericycle performs photosynthesis", "C": "Stem pericycle absorbs soil water", "D": "Root pericycle secretes cuticle"},
         "A", "Pericycle Functional Adaptations.",
         "1. In roots, the pericycle is living parenchyma because it retains totipotency to generate endogenous lateral root primordia and vascular cambium.\n2. In stems, pericycle patches above phloem are sclerenchymatous (hard bast) to provide rigid structural support.",
         "Options B, C, and D are biologically incorrect.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Pericycle Differences"),

        (209, "In an isobilateral leaf, why are stomata present on both adaxial and abaxial epidermises?",
         {"A": "Both leaf surfaces receive almost equal sunlight and air circulation, so gas exchange can occur equally across both sides", "B": "To allow the leaf to float on water", "C": "To absorb minerals from rain droplets", "D": "Because the leaf has no vascular bundles"},
         "A", "Amphistomatic Monocot Leaf Advantage.",
         "1. Isobilateral leaves (grasses, maize, iris) stand nearly vertical.\n2. Both faces receive comparable solar irradiance and wind exposure, making equal stomatal distribution (amphistomatic) optimal for carbon dioxide assimilation.",
         "Options B, C, and D are false.",
         "Medium", "bio-root-stem-leaf-anatomy", "Internal Anatomy of Root, Stem & Leaf", "Amphistomatic Leaf Advantage"),
    ]
